<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Fleming</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Alexander Fleming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Alexander Fleming was a Scottish physician and microbiologist, born at Lochfield farm near Darvel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a>, who spent his career at St Mary's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Serving in the Royal Army Medical Corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, he showed that antiseptics often worsened deep wounds, and in 1922 he discovered the enzyme lysozyme. In 1928 he found that the mould Penicillium rubens produced a substance later named benzylpenicillin, the first antibiotic substance discovered. He was knighted in 1944 and shared the 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/howard-florey">Howard Florey</a> and Ernst Chain. He died of a heart attack at his home in London in 1955, and his ashes are buried in St Paul's Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-fleming.jpg" alt="Alexander Fleming" width="330" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Official photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Synthetic_Production_of_Penicillin_TR1468.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Back from holiday, <b>Alexander Fleming</b> found a mould killing bacteria on a culture plate in his London laboratory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
